YAFAP. Sorry in advance about the length and appalling style.

Bob had a troubled childhood. Teased by the other dwarves because of her
boy’s name, she took solace in digging around in caves on her own, and
studying the ancient lore stored in the dwarves’ library (presided over by
Eva).

One day, delving away she hit a new and strange cave. Interest, she wandered
in. Almost immediately, she found herself surrounded by all manner of
unpleasant creatures. Thankfully, all that digging had made her pretty strong,
so she was able to bash her way through them, with the help of her pet dog,
Deefer.

One of Bob’s studies had been comparative religion, and she knew a lot about
the strange god, RNG. As she progressed through this mysterious cavern, and she
began to feel that she was being played with. Twice he gifted her with magic
lamps, from which she got nice grey dragonhide armour, and the mighty sword
Grayswandir, but not until she reached the hellish depths far later did she
find wands to kill things or change them, nor scrolls to polish  her  sword and
armour nor pens to write them with. But with sturdy Dwarvish stoicism, she
trudged on. Hell, she couldn’t complain. All the while, she seemed to be
getting through pets at an alarming rate: Deefer died, but in his place she
found a water elemental and a horned devil who would do more or less what she
told them. These two were to perish in a big fight in a castle later on.

After a while, she found a strange place full of boulders. After a bit of
pushing, she found a spiffy silver amulet to wear. Another strange diversion,
back to her old college, yielded a crystal ball and a bell. Eventually, she
arrived at the isle of the legendary medusa.. She was very interested in the
historic statues, and even chipped a shield off of one of them to keep as a
souvenir.

Further on, she reached a great castle, and, with the aid of a magic ring that
caused people to fight each other, she emptied it of its defenders. Here, she
found a wand that gave her almost all that she desired. Happy, she descended
into the underworld. With her mighty silver sword, the legions of demons were
no problem: the only hiccup was when putting on the wrong helmet made her mind
change in unusual ways. By the time it was off, she felt that Quezacoatl was
rather less protective of her than before.

Working quickly, she got what she needed to perform a ritual she’d read in
her old books. The ritual done, she entered Moloch’s dread sanctum. The man
himself was out, but she soon got what she wanted anyway: the Amulet of Yendor.
Keeping tight hold on it, she started fleeing for the surface. She kept getting
dragged back by a strange force until she almost went mad, and the wizard that
she thought she’d killed earlier turned up, along with a clone of himself.
She killed them both, and he left her alone for a bit.

She ran straight up through the dungeon, hardly stopping to kill things
(tempting though that  was.) She ran straight through the planes of earth, air,
fire and water too, before eventually arriving on the astral plane. Her
guardian angel died almost immediately, so she decided to try out that funky
ring again: it still worked. She ran to the right, killing Pestilence  with a
wand, only to find that it was the wrong altar. Now she went to the left, past
Famine this time: still wrong. Oh well, she though: time  time for a change of
headgear. Suddenly, the altar she was at looked more attractive: she made
friends with her new God by giving him the amulet.

